    "created_at": "2025-06-24T14:43:03Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-06-24T14:43:03Z",
    "body": "[According to the HTML standard](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#start-tags), the slash on a start tag is unnecessary and has no effect:\n\n> 6. Then, if the element is one of the [void elements](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#void-elements), or if the element is a [foreign element](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#foreign-elements), then there may be a single U+002F SOLIDUS character (/), which on [foreign elements](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#foreign-elements) marks the start tag as self-closing. On [void elements](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#void-elements), it does not mark the start tag as self-closing but instead is unnecessary and has no effect of any kind. For such void elements, it should be used only with caution — especially since, if directly preceded by an [unquoted attribute value](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/syntax.html#unquoted), it becomes part of the attribute value rather than being discarded by the parser.\n\nSo it is either a void element (without an end tag) or it isn't (with an end tag). So having both `<style>foo</style>` and `<style src=\"/styles.css\" />` would be out of the question.\n\nAlso, a `<link>` is a reference to another document that may or may not be used by the user agent. The user agent doesn't have to implement a style, or there may be a selection of styles from which the user agent will choose one. Following `href` is optional.\n\nThis does not sit well with a script that _must_ be executed (assuming the user agent can execute scripts). Following `src` is not optional.\n\nOne is for presentation, the other is for interactivity.\n\nAs for styling, you have 3 methods (in order of precedence):\n\n- **Inline styling:** The `style` attribute for a particular element\n- **Internal styling:** The `<style>` element for a document. Basically, a collection of `style` attributes for the document.\n- **External styling:** A separate CSS document that can be shared among multiple HTML documents. It might have been more consistent to have used `<style href=\"style.css\"></style>`. It might be an idea to add it as an alternative syntax fo a `<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\">` element?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
